  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nA significant pattern shift is still anticipated this weekend \nthrough early next week as the most of the deterministic and \nensemble model guidance continues to show a broad troughing \npattern setting up across much of the western third of the CONUS. \nGuidance continues to show a northern stream trough diving into \nthe Pacific Northwest on Saturday before sinking southward over CA\non Sunday. Meanwhile, a more southern stream shortwave trough is \nlikely to move into the subtropical eastern Pacific and pick up \nthe remnants of Hurricane Rachel as it moves northeastward and \nphases with the west coast trough. As this whole pattern evolution\ntakes shape, the increasing south to southwesterly flow aloft \nwill advect moisture into the Desert Southwest starting Saturday, \nwith most of the moisture at first confined in the mid to upper-\nlevels before better low-level moisture moves in on Sunday as the \nremnants of Rachel gets pulled in. Coincidentally, rain chances \nwill be on the increase as early as Saturday afternoon across \nsouth-central AZ before peaking Sunday into Monday across much of \nthe region with the arrival of better low-level moisture. There \ncontinues to be a great deal of uncertainty in rainfall amounts as\nit will be dependent on the overall track of the remnants of \nRachel and how it interacts with the west coast trough. It will \nlikely take at least another couple of days to get a better idea \non the rainfall perspectives as guidance comes into better \nagreement on how the overall pattern shapes up. In addition to the\nrainfall potential, winds will be another hazard to contend with \nas the combination of the height falls from the trough and remnant\ncirculation of Rachel will likely result in widespread breeziness.\n\nAs 500 mb heights lower with the approaching trough along with \nthe increasing cloud cover and moisture, much cooler temperatures\nare expected this weekend into early next week. Afternoon highs \nacross the lower deserts will go from mid to upper 90s on Saturday\nto low to mid 80s by Monday and may potentially drop even lower \non Tuesday with many locations stuck in the low 80s.",
